1
00:00:01,418 --> 00:00:05,359
[SPEAKER_00]: Prate, can you explain the practice of samatha and vipassana?

2
00:00:06,520 --> 00:00:06,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

3
00:00:08,801 --> 00:00:12,742
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we can start by sort of applying a blanket.

4
00:00:13,422 --> 00:00:17,924
[SPEAKER_00]: Or maybe I just, I will start with some idea which I took yesterday.

5
00:00:17,964 --> 00:00:19,364
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay, yeah, you can do that.

6
00:00:19,725 --> 00:00:22,966
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it was after, you know, talking with you about

7
00:00:25,198 --> 00:00:46,874
[SPEAKER_00]: This phenomenological approach, seeing the peripheral, I was still considering that actually to practice just simple ānāpāna meditation as I used to do it, you know, just relax, staying in the present with your breathing, is actually still a good thing to do in your spare time.

8
00:00:47,694 --> 00:00:51,635
[SPEAKER_00]: But it seems that you have something to say about it.

9
00:00:51,855 --> 00:00:53,576
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I think one... what did I say yesterday?

10
00:00:53,616 --> 00:00:54,296
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, yeah.

11
00:00:54,376 --> 00:01:03,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, basically, most... more often than not, it won't be a good thing, because you won't do it mindfully.

12
00:01:05,179 --> 00:01:11,861
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if you... if you... if you go onto an object of meditation, whatever that might be, and if you want to do it just to sort of

13
00:01:13,322 --> 00:01:14,423
[SPEAKER_01]: Calm down and whatnot.

14
00:01:14,623 --> 00:01:17,145
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, you can do it, but on the basis of mindfulness.

15
00:01:17,485 --> 00:01:24,230
[SPEAKER_01]: And the basis of mindfulness, as we've been discussing all these previous talks, is the basis of the awareness of the peripheral.

16
00:01:25,411 --> 00:01:40,762
[SPEAKER_01]: So, as I said yesterday, sure, you can't possibly sustain, although you could surprise yourself, but the amount of energy you can invest in sort of repetition of this discernment of the peripheral in regard to actual and actual in regard to peripheral,

17
00:01:41,358 --> 00:01:46,160
[SPEAKER_01]: So that you fully, sort of, keep drilling that in, training and drilling, training and drilling.

18
00:01:46,840 --> 00:02:02,345
[SPEAKER_01]: So, but say you're tired of it, so then, yes, you stop actively pursuing the discernment of that principle, but you don't then turn completely away and then remain and become unmindful, become unaware of the principle.

19
00:02:03,446 --> 00:02:04,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that means you're not mindful.

20
00:02:05,026 --> 00:02:09,468
[SPEAKER_01]: That means when you go to meditate, if you meditate without being aware of the principle,

21
00:02:10,262 --> 00:02:14,002
[SPEAKER_01]: You meditate without mindfulness, which means that's not meditation at all.

22
00:02:15,726 --> 00:02:18,207
[SPEAKER_00]: But it can be still a wholesome activity.

23
00:02:18,327 --> 00:02:25,930
[SPEAKER_00]: You know, you are not engaged in sensual desires, you're doing something unwholesome... Well, it's wholesome in a very relative manner, then.

24
00:02:26,371 --> 00:02:29,992
[SPEAKER_01]: It's wholesome because... only because you don't engage in sensual desire.

25
00:02:30,532 --> 00:02:37,715
[SPEAKER_01]: But it remains very much unwholesome, because it won't take you out of the putujana status, nor will it take you out of the wrong view.

26
00:02:38,216 --> 00:02:41,037
[SPEAKER_01]: On the contrary, it will probably keep perpetuating the wrong view.

27
00:02:41,537 --> 00:02:54,704
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha said those meditators, samanas and brāhmaṇas, without the right view, they end up meditating... they think they meditate, but they end up premeditating, overmeditating, mismeditating, undermeditating, and all those kind of permutations.

28
00:02:55,824 --> 00:02:56,285
[SPEAKER_01]: Because why?

29
00:02:56,305 --> 00:03:02,548
[SPEAKER_01]: The mind is still bound with sensuality, with the notions of self, with all the wrong assumptions, and they meditate with that.

30
00:03:03,228 --> 00:03:07,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, a patujana cannot meditate with a pure mind, because he doesn't have such a thing.

31
00:03:08,671 --> 00:03:11,312
[SPEAKER_01]: He doesn't even have a concept of what phenomena the mind is.

32
00:03:11,952 --> 00:03:15,113
[SPEAKER_01]: But he can purify his mind by practicing jhānas.

33
00:03:15,433 --> 00:03:15,993
[SPEAKER_01]: He can do it.

34
00:03:16,133 --> 00:03:16,353
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

35
00:03:16,433 --> 00:03:24,195
[SPEAKER_01]: And jhānas are nothing other than fully established upon the principle of mindfulness, which is the principle we've been discussing, which I think maybe was even recorded.

36
00:03:24,315 --> 00:03:38,158
[SPEAKER_01]: I explained to you that extending the same awareness of the peripheral in regard to... you can go as far as to the kasiṇas, the four great elements, the element of consciousness, which is the most refined one, to all the jhānas, to the immaterial mental establishments.

37
00:03:39,351 --> 00:03:41,693
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the extending the mind along the same principle.

38
00:03:41,713 --> 00:03:57,966
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Sariputin, one by one, that's the Thirumajivinikāya, goes to the first jhāna, and then he kind of, ah, recognizes the factors that are there, and says this is all kind of being held by mindfulness, and then he's there beyond, and then following the same principles, he says, yeah, there is beyond.

39
00:03:58,006 --> 00:04:07,074
[SPEAKER_01]: So then he goes higher, higher, higher, higher, higher, until, obviously, with his right view, he reaches to the cessation of perception and feeling, which is obviously that there is no beyond that

40
00:04:09,336 --> 00:04:15,602
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's just to see how far this this great structure kind of stretches itself.

41
00:04:16,443 --> 00:04:17,183
[SPEAKER_01]: Is there beyond this?

42
00:04:17,224 --> 00:04:17,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, yes, yes.

43
00:04:18,245 --> 00:04:21,668
[SPEAKER_01]: Up to the infinite space, the infinite consciousness, neither perception or no perception.

44
00:04:22,949 --> 00:04:26,533
[SPEAKER_01]: All those things in regard to mind being developed in regard to that peripheral.

45
00:04:27,753 --> 00:04:34,038
[SPEAKER_01]: Because when you... when you're able to sort of reach... and it's not like it's abstract now, because the mind is not developed.

46
00:04:34,518 --> 00:04:41,583
[SPEAKER_01]: But for the developed mind, reaching the... the verges of the phenomenon of space, goes into the infinite space.

47
00:04:42,404 --> 00:04:53,312
[SPEAKER_01]: The verges of the... surmounting the perceptions of unity and diversity, doesn't matter a particular general nature of perception surmounted, you reach a base of neither perception or no perception.

48
00:04:54,675 --> 00:05:10,702
[SPEAKER_01]: So jhānas, that's why the Buddha praised the jhānas, because even... Sure, you don't need necessarily the right view, but in order to do them, you're doing them along the right view, along the right principle, along the principles of pratītyānupāda, along the principles of surmounting sensuality, surmounting the master I am of the experience.

49
00:05:10,962 --> 00:05:18,025
[SPEAKER_01]: Many, so to say, that I am cannot be present in the jhānas, it ceases beforehand, that's why the man enters jhānas and so on.

50
00:05:18,785 --> 00:05:20,047
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why they would appraise them.

51
00:05:20,287 --> 00:05:29,399
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you were to develop jhānas as patrūjanas, then you would need very, very little instruction to to put it all together and become probably an arahant right away.

52
00:05:30,160 --> 00:05:34,787
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the mind is already developed to such an extent that it has surmounted the domain of sensuality.

53
00:05:36,542 --> 00:05:40,906
[SPEAKER_01]: has fully understood the nature of saḷāyātana, and its impermanence, and so on.

54
00:05:41,466 --> 00:05:48,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's very important to recognize that Putujana cannot meditate rightly, unless he's a sotāpanna.

55
00:05:50,774 --> 00:05:52,595
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, that's a strong statement.

56
00:05:52,615 --> 00:05:58,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, that's... Well, you don't need to go further than Samādhīti sutta and see that right view makes everything right.

57
00:05:59,220 --> 00:05:59,461
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

58
00:05:59,701 --> 00:05:59,941
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

59
00:05:59,981 --> 00:06:02,423
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not my statement, it's the whole sutta about it.

60
00:06:02,483 --> 00:06:04,625
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you meditate rightly if you have no right view?

61
00:06:05,425 --> 00:06:06,006
[SPEAKER_01]: And the story?

62
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:11,887
[SPEAKER_00]: But, eh... it can be done without you knowing that you are meditating rightly.

63
00:06:11,908 --> 00:06:14,111
[SPEAKER_00]: You just meditate and prepare your mind.

64
00:06:16,531 --> 00:06:25,234
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you don't know it... Well, if right view is about the knowledge, if liberation is about the knowledge, how can you be knowing while not knowing?

65
00:06:26,094 --> 00:06:26,294
[SPEAKER_01]: i.e.

66
00:06:26,314 --> 00:06:28,935
[SPEAKER_01]: how can you be doing it rightly while not knowing?

67
00:06:29,276 --> 00:06:35,698
[SPEAKER_01]: If you're not knowing... Well, the one who understands those instructions is the Sotāpanna.

68
00:06:36,258 --> 00:06:37,419
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody shorter than that.

69
00:06:38,019 --> 00:06:40,460
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means you follow something you haven't understood.

70
00:06:41,179 --> 00:06:42,180
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how can you follow it?

71
00:06:42,920 --> 00:06:46,842
[SPEAKER_01]: If I give you instruction, and you make no sense out of it, how can you perform it?

72
00:06:47,442 --> 00:06:49,203
[SPEAKER_00]: Because you just follow instructions.

73
00:06:49,223 --> 00:06:49,703
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, no.

74
00:06:49,723 --> 00:06:52,485
[SPEAKER_00]: You give me some task to do, and I don't understand.

75
00:06:52,665 --> 00:06:53,045
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

76
00:06:53,445 --> 00:06:58,268
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you won't do the task rightly, if the task is based upon you understanding the task.

77
00:07:00,123 --> 00:07:06,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the Buddha didn't say just perform these mechanical things and you'll get to the final result.

78
00:07:06,766 --> 00:07:14,270
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, understand what I'm teaching you and then you will be your own kind of leader, your own sort of guide to the final task.

79
00:07:14,770 --> 00:07:14,930
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

80
00:07:15,270 --> 00:07:25,495
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you're sort of talking about would be as if the Buddha gave this impersonal sort of set of methods and instructions that you can just stick to it and you will arrive at it.

81
00:07:26,344 --> 00:07:27,684
[SPEAKER_01]: But he didn't.

82
00:07:27,804 --> 00:07:30,225
[SPEAKER_01]: He did give instructions that need to be understood.

83
00:07:30,625 --> 00:07:32,026
[SPEAKER_01]: Hence, the right view comes first.

84
00:07:32,466 --> 00:07:36,127
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said himself, nirvāṇa-dhīti-sutta, why is saṃsamādhi right?

85
00:07:36,247 --> 00:07:37,468
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of the right view.

86
00:07:37,968 --> 00:07:40,528
[SPEAKER_01]: So, without the right view, you cannot meditate rightly.

87
00:07:40,548 --> 00:07:43,970
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, but, but on the other hand, I'm not saying but, but, but... Yeah, yeah.

88
00:07:44,030 --> 00:07:50,412
[SPEAKER_00]: But on the other hand, we have quite a few examples of people who have jhānas and they're still put to jhānas.

89
00:07:50,452 --> 00:07:51,272
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, that's what I said.

90
00:07:51,936 --> 00:07:59,541
[SPEAKER_01]: So, theoretically, as puthujjana, you can develop jhānas, you can develop the full understanding of this principle and develop your mind.

91
00:07:59,581 --> 00:08:05,484
[SPEAKER_01]: And not necessarily, until somebody tells you externally, turn that to the nature of the sense of self.

92
00:08:06,285 --> 00:08:11,488
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why puthujjana can be free from all the other upādānas except the upādāna of the self.

93
00:08:14,053 --> 00:08:21,519
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a sutta, Bhāgavata, I think it's in Majjhima Nikaya, or maybe in Uttara, not sure, where putujan can be free from sensuality, can be free from sūla-bhāta-paramāsa.

94
00:08:21,999 --> 00:08:25,482
[SPEAKER_01]: He cannot be free from the self-view.

95
00:08:26,523 --> 00:08:29,365
[SPEAKER_01]: Because for that, you need utterance of another.

96
00:08:30,466 --> 00:08:34,229
[SPEAKER_01]: But he will have very much of ayoniso manasikāra if he had jhānas.

97
00:08:34,749 --> 00:08:42,335
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like you say jhānas, the four jhānas, but then you have even kind of more refined immaterial attainments.

98
00:08:42,715 --> 00:08:46,918
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why when the Buddha became the Buddha, when the Brahma pleaded to him to go and teach,

99
00:08:48,237 --> 00:08:48,977
[SPEAKER_01]: Who should I teach?

100
00:08:49,458 --> 00:08:54,259
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they're all kutujanas, but which ones have the least dust in their eyes?

101
00:08:54,840 --> 00:08:59,481
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he turned to his previous teacher, not out of some traditional gratitude or anything like that.

102
00:08:59,561 --> 00:09:01,682
[SPEAKER_01]: No, because he had the least dust in his eyes.

103
00:09:02,002 --> 00:09:04,123
[SPEAKER_01]: He was the basis of neither perception nor perception.

104
00:09:04,683 --> 00:09:12,566
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize, as a kutujana, he was really, really... he'd done 99.9% of work that arahant needs to do.

105
00:09:13,346 --> 00:09:18,071
[SPEAKER_01]: All he needed is just that one, 0.1%, to set everything rightly, that he already had.

106
00:09:18,631 --> 00:09:21,814
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when they told him, the Devas told him, he died, he said, why?

107
00:09:21,875 --> 00:09:23,496
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a really great loss for him.

108
00:09:23,636 --> 00:09:28,401
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, unimaginable loss, because he actually was so, so close to this very thing.

109
00:09:28,921 --> 00:09:37,009
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, he went down the next one, which is the base of, uh, infinite space, whatever it was, all for the purpose, because they had, they'd done all the work, mostly.

110
00:09:37,857 --> 00:09:41,798
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why when you do have people in the sūtras, they heard just one line from the Buddha and became arahants.

111
00:09:41,878 --> 00:09:44,019
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because they've done all the work rightly.

112
00:09:44,439 --> 00:09:49,240
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they developed the whole understanding that resulted in the whole freedom of upādāna and everything.

113
00:09:49,260 --> 00:09:50,561
[SPEAKER_01]: They just needed that last drop.

114
00:09:50,981 --> 00:09:53,162
[SPEAKER_01]: So, they did a lot of samarāyaṭa understanding.

115
00:09:53,382 --> 00:09:53,642
[SPEAKER_01]: Really?

116
00:09:53,782 --> 00:09:54,242
[SPEAKER_01]: Understanding.

117
00:09:54,282 --> 00:09:54,862
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, of course.

118
00:09:55,722 --> 00:09:55,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

119
00:09:56,303 --> 00:10:00,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if Buddha comes to you and tells you, in seeing, let there be the seeing.

120
00:10:00,724 --> 00:10:02,304
[SPEAKER_01]: In heard, let there only be the heard.

121
00:10:02,964 --> 00:10:04,585
[SPEAKER_01]: And you understand that right there and then.

122
00:10:05,684 --> 00:10:10,465
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't be able to understand that if you have many wrong views blocking it, covering it up.

123
00:10:11,485 --> 00:10:26,148
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you clear all the wrong views, but you're not still quite there yet, and then somebody tells you that, and you understand it as clear as a day, as they tell to you, you can only do that if the mind is able to to sort of reach it at that level.

124
00:10:26,788 --> 00:10:28,789
[SPEAKER_01]: And it can reach that level if it made the right effort.

125
00:10:28,809 --> 00:10:29,549
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, again,

126
00:10:31,621 --> 00:10:43,143
[SPEAKER_01]: You should keep trying to meditate and keep trying to sort of understand along the lines of understanding, not along the lines of mechanical technique, which you hope is purifying your mind somewhere down the line.

127
00:10:43,744 --> 00:10:50,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Because even if it were, you have no right criteria, you don't have right view to recognize what the actual purity is.

128
00:10:50,765 --> 00:10:53,185
[SPEAKER_01]: So, even if it sits on your face, you won't recognize it.

129
00:10:53,706 --> 00:10:54,486
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah.

130
00:10:54,666 --> 00:10:59,707
[SPEAKER_00]: I, I, it seems that I can agree with this statement, that last one you said,

131
00:11:01,149 --> 00:11:07,611
[SPEAKER_00]: But, I will show you my understanding, which I used to, for which I have.

132
00:11:08,251 --> 00:11:11,692
[SPEAKER_00]: I was thinking that you purify your mind from nirvana.

133
00:11:12,533 --> 00:11:19,135
[SPEAKER_00]: And by purifying your mind from nirvana, you remove the obstacles, and then you can create.

134
00:11:19,155 --> 00:11:28,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, sure, that's not wrong statement to make, but purifying your mind of nirvana is done by understanding what nirvana are.

135
00:11:29,533 --> 00:11:32,117
[SPEAKER_01]: By full understanding the nature, you surmount it.

136
00:11:32,878 --> 00:11:36,944
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Mahārāja Moggallānā entered the first jhāna, by understanding what the first jhāna was.

137
00:11:37,645 --> 00:11:38,847
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by doing this or that.

138
00:11:39,790 --> 00:11:48,834
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can we say that actually overcoming... you overcome nivārāṇa by understanding what nivārāṇa is, means that you don't overcome them by technique, by just practicing some meditation?

139
00:11:48,854 --> 00:11:49,134
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

140
00:11:49,214 --> 00:11:49,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Exactly.

141
00:12:00,618 --> 00:12:03,300
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you overcome by understanding that.

142
00:12:03,620 --> 00:12:05,621
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how the mind develops in regard to it.

143
00:12:06,001 --> 00:12:23,472
[SPEAKER_01]: And understanding can take place only on the basis of principle of tatticamāpāda, the basic principle of understanding that a reason phenomenon as it has a reason, which means on the basis of mindfulness, which means on the basis of discerning peripheral in regard to the actual or actual in regard to peripheral.

144
00:12:24,677 --> 00:12:28,900
[SPEAKER_01]: Because nivāraṇas are also phenomena that are manifested in your experience as a whole.

145
00:12:29,780 --> 00:12:34,023
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you understand it there, on the basis of the experience as a whole, you remove the ownership of it.

146
00:12:34,083 --> 00:12:39,967
[SPEAKER_01]: You remain... you remove, sort of, their influence over you, by not owning them.

147
00:12:40,487 --> 00:12:40,747
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

148
00:12:40,907 --> 00:12:44,089
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you give them power by taking possession of them.

149
00:12:45,330 --> 00:12:46,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh.

150
00:12:46,391 --> 00:12:53,535
[SPEAKER_00]: And what about, eh... eh... sotāpāna, and then sakadāgāmī, anagāmī, they still have...

151
00:12:55,184 --> 00:12:57,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, to an extent.

152
00:12:57,925 --> 00:12:58,545
[SPEAKER_01]: To an extent.

153
00:12:59,486 --> 00:13:00,666
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you have understood it.

154
00:13:00,786 --> 00:13:07,008
[SPEAKER_01]: But, as I said before, even with this principle that you can kind of understand, you still didn't fully understand it, didn't you?

155
00:13:07,248 --> 00:13:08,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't repeated it long enough.

156
00:13:09,269 --> 00:13:13,250
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why an ārya, who is still not an ārahant, still has work to do.

157
00:13:14,158 --> 00:13:22,605
[SPEAKER_01]: He still needs to keep re-engaging in repeating of the same principle, of the same understanding that he has, which is why nobody else can be his guide.

158
00:13:22,905 --> 00:13:23,946
[SPEAKER_01]: Not even Buddha.

159
00:13:24,326 --> 00:13:24,906
[SPEAKER_01]: He himself.

160
00:13:25,127 --> 00:13:29,610
[SPEAKER_01]: He got a criteria, he got a principle, he got a base for it, he got an understanding.

161
00:13:30,010 --> 00:13:31,371
[SPEAKER_01]: He just needs to keep drilling it.

162
00:13:31,592 --> 00:13:32,752
[SPEAKER_01]: So, just Buddha at first?

163
00:13:32,772 --> 00:13:33,573
[SPEAKER_01]: Just keep drilling it.

164
00:13:33,613 --> 00:13:33,973
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

165
00:13:33,993 --> 00:13:38,057
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the Buddha Vipassi, before he became Samasambuddha,

166
00:13:38,797 --> 00:13:40,618
[SPEAKER_01]: He discerned paticca-sampada.

167
00:13:41,238 --> 00:13:49,522
[SPEAKER_01]: And in that, and in Kāyā-sutta, he describes paticca-sampada, and he comes to the point of nāmarūpa determines viññāṇa, and viññāṇa determines nāmarūpa.

168
00:13:49,562 --> 00:13:51,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he doesn't define paticca-sampada further.

169
00:13:52,403 --> 00:14:06,710
[SPEAKER_01]: And then he says, by understanding that, he remained contemplating manifestation arising and ceasing of the five aggregates, of the experience as a whole, until his mind was purified from all of the, all of the kilesas, and he became sama-sambuddha.

170
00:14:07,452 --> 00:14:10,073
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if he understood the principle, then just remain drilling it.

171
00:14:10,393 --> 00:14:12,673
[SPEAKER_01]: And it doesn't say he remained drilling Pāṭhita-saṃpāda.

172
00:14:13,134 --> 00:14:22,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It says he understood Pāṭhita-saṃpāda, nāmarūpa, viññāṇa, viññāṇa, nāmarūpa, and then he remained just being mindful of arising and falling of the five aggregates.

173
00:14:23,056 --> 00:14:31,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Of the enduring of the five aggregates, of persisting, of changing, of the five aggregates being independent of any sort of notion of mine and myself.

174
00:14:32,699 --> 00:14:37,902
[SPEAKER_01]: He fully drilled that, to the extent necessary, for the final knowledge, and with that insight, everything was done.

175
00:14:38,542 --> 00:14:47,087
[SPEAKER_01]: So, can we say that, basically, the essence, the point is to produce that structure of experience, and then... Understanding.

176
00:14:47,167 --> 00:14:47,808
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the point.

177
00:14:48,128 --> 00:14:49,249
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why, like, I mean...

178
00:14:50,515 --> 00:14:53,077
[SPEAKER_01]: It's kind of unfortunate that this needs to be said.

179
00:14:53,757 --> 00:14:57,360
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like to be particular, but generally, because, yeah, many people...

180
00:14:57,380 --> 00:15:03,244
[SPEAKER_01]: I mean, you read so much in every sutta, it's just like the final knowledge, the final knowledge, the final understanding.

181
00:15:04,024 --> 00:15:05,865
[SPEAKER_01]: Putujana can relate to knowledge.

182
00:15:06,186 --> 00:15:07,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Just think about the phenomenon of knowledge.

183
00:15:08,007 --> 00:15:09,488
[SPEAKER_01]: It means when you know something.

184
00:15:10,308 --> 00:15:15,652
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, you must not abandon that notion of knowledge when you're pursuing the way out of suffering.

185
00:15:17,456 --> 00:15:29,849
[SPEAKER_01]: As appealing as it is to give in to any personal mechanical technique that sort of numbs you out for a while, makes you very peaceful, it's a very, very dangerous path to take, because you become dependent upon it.

186
00:15:29,870 --> 00:15:32,092
[SPEAKER_01]: That pleasure, that forgetfulness, that oblivion.

187
00:15:33,596 --> 00:15:37,118
[SPEAKER_01]: So, initially, everything seems painful, because you want to know everything, but you can't.

188
00:15:37,619 --> 00:15:43,222
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's on the basis of that, that you can actually discern important from unimportant, which is an undescription for Sotāpanna.

189
00:15:43,742 --> 00:15:45,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I, what's important knowledge, what it isn't.

190
00:15:46,144 --> 00:15:46,644
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's still...

191
00:15:47,105 --> 00:15:48,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry, but it still is the knowledge.

192
00:15:50,607 --> 00:15:55,390
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to answer your original question about samatha and vipassana,

193
00:15:56,175 --> 00:16:04,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Vipassana is actively pursuing the discernment of those principles, whatever approach you take, the five aggregates, vipaṁ, upādāna, or peripheral and actual.

194
00:16:04,977 --> 00:16:13,540
[SPEAKER_01]: Anything that's based on mindfulness, that's where you cannot have too much of mindfulness, and that's where mindfulness is always first, basically, in whatever list they come up.

195
00:16:15,041 --> 00:16:23,624
[SPEAKER_01]: So, vipassana would be actively pursuing the discernment, while samatha would be not actively pursuing the discernment, but maintaining the basis for discernment.

196
00:16:23,724 --> 00:16:23,984
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

197
00:16:24,525 --> 00:16:33,988
[SPEAKER_01]: See, if you maintain the basis for discernment, but not exerting yourself in understanding, you will progress a bit slower in terms of understanding, but nevertheless, you eventually, you start seeing those things.

198
00:16:34,068 --> 00:16:34,288
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

199
00:16:34,308 --> 00:16:39,930
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you know what you're supposed to see, and you keep holding it in front of you, even if you're not necessarily trying to see.

200
00:16:40,531 --> 00:16:43,912
[SPEAKER_01]: You still hold all the components rightly in front of you,

201
00:16:44,412 --> 00:16:46,953
[SPEAKER_01]: In samatha, that's why samatha carries vipassana.

202
00:16:47,653 --> 00:16:57,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Vice versa, actively pursuing vipassana means you composing that principle and keeping it in front of you, means vipassana is dragging samatha with it.

203
00:16:58,355 --> 00:17:00,635
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's not optional, if done rightly.

204
00:17:00,855 --> 00:17:02,115
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's like the two yokes.

205
00:17:02,596 --> 00:17:04,336
[SPEAKER_01]: Yoke to the two, two, two oxes.

206
00:17:04,376 --> 00:17:07,917
[SPEAKER_01]: So, one might go slightly forward, another one might... That doesn't matter.

207
00:17:08,397 --> 00:17:10,037
[SPEAKER_01]: They will both be going forward.

208
00:17:11,856 --> 00:17:26,802
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you engage in any form of samatha, that is not based on mindfulness, not based on maintaining the same principles, even if you don't necessarily actively pursue them, well, you have no basis for any vipassana, which means that's not samatha.

209
00:17:27,662 --> 00:17:28,723
[SPEAKER_01]: That's patujana samatha.

210
00:17:28,743 --> 00:17:31,764
[SPEAKER_01]: That's his premeditation, overmeditation, undermeditation, and so on.

211
00:17:32,764 --> 00:17:38,826
[SPEAKER_01]: And that cannot possibly, magically, sort of transfer you into the right, right way of meditating.

212
00:17:39,647 --> 00:17:40,207
[SPEAKER_01]: You just couldn't.

213
00:17:43,851 --> 00:17:49,917
[SPEAKER_00]: Mindfulness is in common to vipassana and to samatha.

214
00:17:49,937 --> 00:17:55,001
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why every jhāna is done properly, which is what jhāna is when it's done properly.

215
00:18:07,128 --> 00:18:10,130
[SPEAKER_01]: It has mindfulness as its basis.

216
00:18:10,150 --> 00:18:15,033
[SPEAKER_01]: And you read one by one sutra, you read other sutras, mindfulness is present in every jhāna.

217
00:18:15,734 --> 00:18:19,636
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it must be basically founded upon it.

218
00:18:19,776 --> 00:18:31,564
[SPEAKER_01]: So, instead of thinking what kind of contemporary sort of Buddhist schools think and teach, that meditation is some sort of concentration that makes you focus upon something, that you forget everything else.

219
00:18:32,508 --> 00:18:33,849
[SPEAKER_01]: Think about meditation.

220
00:18:34,489 --> 00:18:36,670
[SPEAKER_01]: Samādhi literally means composure.

221
00:18:37,651 --> 00:18:41,473
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're composing something, you need components for that.

222
00:18:41,513 --> 00:18:42,874
[SPEAKER_01]: Something needs to be composed.

223
00:18:43,174 --> 00:18:45,175
[SPEAKER_01]: Something needs to be kept there, composed.

224
00:18:46,076 --> 00:18:46,776
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's your mind.

225
00:18:46,916 --> 00:18:50,218
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the principle, the understanding of mindfulness that we spoke about before.

226
00:18:51,399 --> 00:18:54,060
[SPEAKER_01]: So, think of samādhi or samatha...

227
00:18:57,002 --> 00:19:01,004
[SPEAKER_01]: Think of samādhi or samatha as unwavering mindfulness.

228
00:19:02,042 --> 00:19:05,384
[SPEAKER_01]: Not actively engaging on the basis of that mindfulness, just being mindful.

229
00:19:05,904 --> 00:19:07,245
[SPEAKER_01]: So, mindfulness enduring.

230
00:19:07,546 --> 00:19:09,927
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindfulness persisting.

231
00:19:10,087 --> 00:19:12,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like it doesn't change at all.

232
00:19:12,128 --> 00:19:14,130
[SPEAKER_01]: Changes while remaining the same.

233
00:19:15,070 --> 00:19:17,512
[SPEAKER_01]: Because mindfulness is another phenomenon as well.

234
00:19:17,812 --> 00:19:20,474
[SPEAKER_01]: Mindful of experience as a whole, which is the phenomenon that is present.

235
00:19:20,514 --> 00:19:26,818
[SPEAKER_00]: Is it not to be like focused on one particular thing, but just to... Actually, peripheral means

236
00:19:27,665 --> 00:19:31,208
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, basically, from one point of view, you see both ends.

237
00:19:31,789 --> 00:19:33,430
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to chase them.

238
00:19:33,631 --> 00:19:38,915
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't have to step into the peripheral and forget about actual and chase actual and forget about peripheral.

239
00:19:39,396 --> 00:19:41,738
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, the other one, what was the...

240
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:48,822
[SPEAKER_01]: That simile... Well, think about the other simile, basically, which can serve the purpose, like a gatekeeper.

241
00:19:49,022 --> 00:19:51,442
[SPEAKER_01]: You don't chase people outside of the city, nor inside the city.

242
00:19:51,462 --> 00:19:56,884
[SPEAKER_01]: You just stay at one point from which you know whoever passes here is inside, whoever goes out is outside.

243
00:19:57,004 --> 00:19:57,924
[SPEAKER_01]: That's all you need to know.

244
00:19:58,984 --> 00:20:01,305
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, that's how you establish your mindfulness.

245
00:20:02,205 --> 00:20:11,548
[SPEAKER_01]: Not by chasing the particular sensations or whatever perceptions, but by being mindful of the basis of them.

246
00:20:13,228 --> 00:20:13,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, whatever.

247
00:20:13,768 --> 00:20:15,229
[SPEAKER_01]: And they can change as fast as they want.

248
00:20:15,909 --> 00:20:16,869
[SPEAKER_01]: You won't lose your mindfulness.

249
00:20:17,429 --> 00:20:17,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

250
00:20:17,709 --> 00:20:27,873
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't based your mindfulness on such a fleeting, particular notion, but on a more general, fundamental recognition of the experience as a whole, which is a basis for these things changing.

251
00:20:28,033 --> 00:20:29,193
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't care how they change.

252
00:20:29,233 --> 00:20:31,054
[SPEAKER_01]: You know they'll have to change inside of this city.

253
00:20:31,554 --> 00:20:32,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they've entered.

254
00:20:32,314 --> 00:20:32,954
[SPEAKER_01]: They have arisen.

255
00:20:33,635 --> 00:20:34,195
[SPEAKER_01]: They've ceased.

256
00:20:34,275 --> 00:20:34,655
[SPEAKER_01]: They're gone.

257
00:20:34,935 --> 00:20:36,155
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, they're not my concern.

258
00:20:36,556 --> 00:20:36,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

259
00:20:38,796 --> 00:20:41,097
[SPEAKER_00]: And what about wholesome and unwholesome?

260
00:20:44,424 --> 00:21:08,289
[SPEAKER_01]: In order to get sort of this kind of mindfulness established, developed and established, unwholesome habits, unwholesome behavior, unwholesome engaging with unwholesome need to have been already kept at bay.

261
00:21:10,504 --> 00:21:13,866
[SPEAKER_01]: Obviously, sometimes you slip, you engage in unwholesome.

262
00:21:14,306 --> 00:21:20,609
[SPEAKER_01]: If you have right mindfulness, it will be quicker for you to kind of readjust, and reset, and start doing things rightly.

263
00:21:21,390 --> 00:21:28,053
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the case for an ārya, who can quickly pick back, pick up the training back, and do it even after a slip.

264
00:21:29,794 --> 00:21:32,756
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for Pratyūjana, he needs to purify his behavior beforehand.

265
00:21:33,256 --> 00:21:34,437
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to have been engaging

266
00:21:35,672 --> 00:21:38,555
[SPEAKER_01]: In a pure behavior, in a sense of not giving in to the unwholesome.

267
00:21:38,976 --> 00:21:47,204
[SPEAKER_01]: And as we spoke a few days ago, that unwholesome, yeah, there is the obvious unwholesome, but often that unwholesome is actually determined by the state of your mind.

268
00:21:47,765 --> 00:21:49,006
[SPEAKER_01]: Whether it's rooted in desire,

269
00:21:50,019 --> 00:21:53,323
[SPEAKER_01]: In craving, or if it's not routine desire, in craving.

270
00:21:53,383 --> 00:21:53,623
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

271
00:21:54,344 --> 00:21:56,846
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's what you become aware of.

272
00:21:57,187 --> 00:22:00,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Not like, kind of, oh, well, everybody says this is fine, and I'll do it.

273
00:22:01,191 --> 00:22:01,431
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

274
00:22:01,491 --> 00:22:01,692
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

275
00:22:01,992 --> 00:22:02,132
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

276
00:22:02,172 --> 00:22:04,715
[SPEAKER_01]: You know you'll be doing it for the wrong reasons.

277
00:22:04,735 --> 00:22:04,895
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

278
00:22:04,955 --> 00:22:10,000
[SPEAKER_01]: For the reasons of pursuing distraction, carelessness, whatever, and feeling justified about it.

279
00:22:10,041 --> 00:22:10,441
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

280
00:22:10,461 --> 00:22:11,322
[SPEAKER_01]: That needs to go.

281
00:22:11,682 --> 00:22:12,023
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

282
00:22:12,563 --> 00:22:17,884
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, these things can take take root, if you obviously apply the effort.

283
00:22:18,224 --> 00:22:23,065
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why the sūtas say that he practiced restraint by the rules of padamagga.

284
00:22:23,085 --> 00:22:25,245
[SPEAKER_01]: He sees the danger in the slightest fault.

285
00:22:26,005 --> 00:22:26,245
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

286
00:22:26,845 --> 00:22:34,567
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means, basically, if you recognize the danger in the slightest giving in to the desire, essentially, that you are not following the teaching.

287
00:22:34,667 --> 00:22:36,027
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't mean you don't do anything.

288
00:22:36,267 --> 00:22:40,328
[SPEAKER_01]: It means you just do things that are not rooted in your unwholesome desire.

289
00:22:41,668 --> 00:22:41,848
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

290
00:22:42,747 --> 00:22:54,870
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you want to go for a walk, because, eh... well, because you're basically feel too lazy to read the suttas, or maybe, although you can, they're right in front of you, you want to go for a walk, and then, that's okay.

291
00:22:55,350 --> 00:22:56,531
[SPEAKER_01]: It is a compromise, you can see.

292
00:22:57,331 --> 00:23:04,993
[SPEAKER_01]: Going for a walk, depending, like, you want to go into the wilderness, well, that's not necessarily... you're not gonna have any kind of sensual distractions there.

293
00:23:05,933 --> 00:23:11,555
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you could sort of do that compromise, because it's quite likely, in the wilderness, your mind will go back to the dhamma.

294
00:23:12,461 --> 00:23:14,702
[SPEAKER_01]: But, say, you go for a walk and you choose to go to the village.

295
00:23:15,482 --> 00:23:19,644
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize, well, you will be meeting people, you will be engaging with it.

296
00:23:20,044 --> 00:23:26,626
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you might not give rise to any seriously unwholesome desire, or an offense, or something like that.

297
00:23:26,726 --> 00:23:34,769
[SPEAKER_01]: But, nevertheless, you're going for the purpose of socializing, turning your mind away from the practice, giving in to distraction of others.

298
00:23:34,930 --> 00:23:35,970
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's unwholesome.

299
00:23:38,578 --> 00:23:42,860
[SPEAKER_01]: If, for example, you want to go to the wilderness because you want to practice further, then it's same.

300
00:23:42,920 --> 00:23:47,503
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, you go for the same walk, but now it's placed in wholesome.

301
00:23:47,623 --> 00:23:48,744
[SPEAKER_01]: It's done for good reason.

302
00:23:49,305 --> 00:23:51,386
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you become responsible for rooting it.

303
00:23:51,886 --> 00:23:53,888
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, like, you live in the monastery, you live in a big temple.

304
00:23:53,988 --> 00:23:58,911
[SPEAKER_01]: Most, like, the temples, they have libraries, Buddhist libraries, every book you want.

305
00:23:59,731 --> 00:24:07,816
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're in your room, bored on the pressure, the dread starts to come up, anxiety, you're a bit tired, you can't keep making the effort.

306
00:24:08,396 --> 00:24:14,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So, well, you know, it's obvious you'll feel too guilty if you just go to the sala or reception room where people are.

307
00:24:15,220 --> 00:24:17,582
[SPEAKER_01]: But no, you go to the library to get the book.

308
00:24:18,302 --> 00:24:26,527
[SPEAKER_01]: But deep down, or not even that deep down, just underneath that, you hope that you will meet people in library to get somebody to talk to and get your mind off things.

309
00:24:27,929 --> 00:24:31,830
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, if you say to him, I'm going to the library to take some suttas, nobody will criticize that.

310
00:24:31,850 --> 00:24:34,770
[SPEAKER_01]: Everyone will say, yeah, very well, he's really making the effort.

311
00:24:35,171 --> 00:24:37,991
[SPEAKER_01]: But you know that actually you're doing it for completely wrong reasons.

312
00:24:38,771 --> 00:24:39,691
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then, you shouldn't do it.

313
00:24:40,092 --> 00:24:40,312
[SPEAKER_00]: Yes.

314
00:24:40,372 --> 00:24:41,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Because there will be a fault.

315
00:24:41,852 --> 00:24:46,813
[SPEAKER_00]: But sometimes you don't know, actually, because you... Well, you have to know.

316
00:24:46,933 --> 00:24:54,355
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not necessarily... You don't need a great wisdom of a sotāpanna or an arahant to discern that.

317
00:24:54,455 --> 00:24:56,235
[SPEAKER_01]: You just need to be honest to yourself.

318
00:24:57,255 --> 00:25:03,798
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why that... the mundane right view is required for any other right view to take place.

319
00:25:04,258 --> 00:25:10,821
[SPEAKER_01]: That recognition, that kind of admitting things to yourself, being transparent to yourself, what is universal, being authentic.

320
00:25:11,462 --> 00:25:13,483
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you recognize, why am I going to London?

321
00:25:13,503 --> 00:25:14,723
[SPEAKER_01]: What is the main reason?

322
00:25:16,284 --> 00:25:21,686
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, like, once you start developing the transparency, that's... that's where the, you know, the contemplation of the food.

323
00:25:22,046 --> 00:25:23,827
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you can't help but eat.

324
00:25:23,947 --> 00:25:25,588
[SPEAKER_01]: You have to eat, because otherwise you'll die.

325
00:25:26,418 --> 00:25:33,940
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you realize then, you can eat for unwholesome reasons or you can make the effort and re-root the reasons for your eating that are necessary.

326
00:25:34,900 --> 00:25:37,661
[SPEAKER_01]: Root them forcefully, if you have to, in wholesome.

327
00:25:38,441 --> 00:25:41,722
[SPEAKER_01]: Don't give rise to the desire and root them in wholesome.

328
00:25:41,742 --> 00:25:45,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Root them in the purpose of your practice, purpose of this life, purpose of your commitment.

329
00:25:46,103 --> 00:25:47,143
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you eat.

330
00:25:47,643 --> 00:25:51,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, to remain healthy, affliction-free, so that you can practice.

331
00:25:52,044 --> 00:25:54,485
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not like, oh, eating is bad or eating is good.

332
00:25:55,285 --> 00:25:57,046
[SPEAKER_01]: You make it so.

333
00:25:57,426 --> 00:26:11,995
[SPEAKER_01]: If you remain mindful of the... and if you are mindful of these more general, vague reasons why you eat, like, oh, to become an arahant, and to do this, and to be able to practice, and to be able to apply myself, the desire can't creep in.

334
00:26:12,396 --> 00:26:20,541
[SPEAKER_01]: If you become unmindful, it goes back to the default, which is with the grain, which is with the flood, which is with the desire, with the taste, with the finesse.

335
00:26:21,950 --> 00:26:24,892
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in the same sense, sometimes you might actually have to go to the library.

336
00:26:25,112 --> 00:26:33,778
[SPEAKER_01]: You really want to get these suttas, but you've been carelessly attending to that, unmindfully, and the real reason becomes meeting other people.

337
00:26:35,599 --> 00:26:37,560
[SPEAKER_01]: So, then you have to re-route it.

338
00:26:37,881 --> 00:26:42,304
[SPEAKER_01]: Then you have to keep bringing back the other reason for getting the sutta books.

339
00:26:43,164 --> 00:26:49,488
[SPEAKER_01]: And then go to the library, and if you do meet other people, you may therefore end up engaging with them, avoiding them, taking the books, and getting out.

340
00:26:52,068 --> 00:26:56,850
[SPEAKER_00]: Is this sīla-bhāta-bhāra-matsar?

341
00:26:57,070 --> 00:27:14,555
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, if you fully develop this principle of transparency, paired with the understanding of the basis of mindfulness, that would reveal the sign of the mind, the notion of the phenomenon of the mind, then you will recognize exactly where the purpose of sīla, the virtue, and bhāta, the duties, is.

342
00:27:14,955 --> 00:27:19,477
[SPEAKER_01]: You will recognize it, and you wouldn't need to hold on to it, because you took responsibility for the whole thing.

343
00:27:20,858 --> 00:27:24,782
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you don't recognize it, that's when people say, no, no, no, but it's always good to go and take the suttas.

344
00:27:24,822 --> 00:27:26,044
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always good to be virtuous.

345
00:27:26,064 --> 00:27:27,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, it is always good to be virtuous.

346
00:27:27,826 --> 00:27:31,069
[SPEAKER_01]: It is always good to read the suttas.

347
00:27:31,610 --> 00:27:34,053
[SPEAKER_01]: But a putujana's mind can make even that unwholesome.

348
00:27:36,189 --> 00:27:41,830
[SPEAKER_01]: If it doesn't know that, fundamentally, wholesome and unwholesome is rooted in desire.

349
00:27:42,631 --> 00:27:44,171
[SPEAKER_01]: So, unwholesome is always rooted in desire.

350
00:27:44,711 --> 00:27:57,094
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, in hindsight, you can recognize that, oh yes, of course, sensuality of any kind, anger, ill will of any kind, cruelty of any kind, it's always rooted in desire.

351
00:27:57,574 --> 00:27:59,895
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot reroute the reasons for that.

352
00:28:00,703 --> 00:28:02,504
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you stop doing them beforehand.

353
00:28:02,564 --> 00:28:06,185
[SPEAKER_01]: You take that on trust, so that you can develop that understanding.

354
00:28:06,445 --> 00:28:09,146
[SPEAKER_00]: Not just so that you can blindly adhere to it for the rest of your life.

355
00:28:09,486 --> 00:28:09,766
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes.

356
00:28:09,846 --> 00:28:14,368
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that will not serve as a basis for wisdom and knowledge that we spoke about.

357
00:28:15,488 --> 00:28:18,389
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that sense, the behavior needs to be purified beforehand.

358
00:28:19,169 --> 00:28:29,153
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, obviously, the practice of the discernment of the principle, the composure of the principle, and the kind of the knowledge of the principle, eventually, can take the place.

